--- MODEL OUTPUT ---
Morning light slips through the cracked shutter of the Broken Lantern Inn and lands across your face. You blink awake on a straw mattress that crackles under your weight. The dream that clung to you seconds ago is already dissolving, leaving only the taste of ash and a faint ringing in your ears.

You sit up. Rough pine walls sweat last night’s hearth-smoke. A clay cup waits on the bedside table, half-full of water gone flat. You reach for it. The cup skates two finger-widths toward you, then stops the instant your knuckles brush the rim. You stare. It sits motionless, as if it never moved.

Boots clomp in the alley below. A woman hawks eggs, her voice rising over iron cart wheels and the yap of someone’s dog. Something wooden topples, followed by bright laughter and the single clang of a handbell. The day outside is already trading, bargaining, living.

The blanket slides off as you swing your legs to the floor. Your clothes are where you left them, still smelling of last night’s rain. Whatever just happened with the cup can wait. The market is awake, and so are you.

